> Here is a consolidated qualifier question that retains a single central task while giving the student room to synthesize across the readings:
> 
> > ## Climate Risk, Adaptation, and Agricultural Transformation among Smallholders
> >
> > Smallholder households facing climate risk can respond through on-farm adaptation (such as changing inputs, varieties, crop mixes, or production practices), diversification and migration, and longer-run reallocation of agricultural activity across crops, land uses, and places. These responses may be enabled or constrained by remittances and other financial flows, social remittances, labor availability and gender norms, market and institutional frictions, and the timing and severity of climatic shocks. They can also produce unequal, indirect, or environmentally maladaptive outcomes.
> >
> > **Drawing on the assigned literature, explain how physical climate risks can shape smallholder adaptation and agricultural transformation or redistribution, and critically assess how these processes can be studied empirically.** In your answer:
> >
> > 1. develop a conceptual account of the relevant adaptation pathways and their interactions, including migration, financial and social remittances, labor allocation, technology adoption, and land-use or crop changes;
> > 2. identify the principal challenges to estimating causal effects and adaptation limits or potential—including endogeneity, selection, measurement, temporal dynamics, and indirect or multi-scalar effects; and
> > 3. propose a credible empirical research design (or complementary set of methods) to address these challenges, specifying the data, identifying assumptions, and limits of inference. Discuss how the resulting evidence could distinguish privately optimal adaptation from adaptation constrained by frictions, and inform equitable and environmentally sustainable policy.
> 
> This formulation has a few advantages:
> 
> - It gives the student **one coherent object of explanation**: the climate-risk-to-household-decision-to-land-system pathway.
> - It makes methodology central, as in your proposed version, but prevents an answer that is merely a catalogue of identification problems.
> - It naturally accommodates the contrast between **ex post coping**, **ex ante investment**, and **long-run structural reallocation**.
> - It makes room for the literature’s core substantive tensions: remittances can relax credit constraints but migration can reduce farm labor; adaptation can reduce losses but remain incomplete; and household responses can create distributional and environmental externalities.
> - Asking for a research design requires the student to move from critique to constructive methodological reasoning.
> 
> If you want a slightly tighter version—better suited to a timed written qualifier—I would use this:
> 
> > ## Climate Risk, Migration, and Smallholder Agricultural Adaptation
> >
> > Climate risk affects smallholder agriculture not only through biophysical impacts on yields, but also through household decisions about production, labor, migration, investment, and land use. These decisions may be shaped by remittances, social networks and knowledge flows, gendered labor constraints, credit and insurance frictions, and the character of climatic shocks.  
> >
> > **Drawing on the assigned readings, evaluate how researchers can credibly estimate the role of climate risk in shaping smallholder adaptation and longer-run agricultural transformation or redistribution.** Develop a conceptual framework linking climate shocks to on-farm adaptation, migration and remittances, and land-use or livelihood outcomes. Then assess the principal empirical challenges—including endogeneity, selection, measurement, heterogeneous responses, and long-run or spatial spillovers—and propose an empirical strategy using appropriate combinations of household, climatic, administrative, and geospatial data. Explain what the proposed strategy can and cannot reveal about adaptation potential, constraints, equity, and environmental sustainability.
> 
> I would favor the second version for a qualifier. It is broad enough to test synthesis of all eight readings, but bounded enough that the student must make choices about a defensible empirical design rather than treating every possible adaptation policy or outcome superficially.
