> I agree on all three points. The question should specify the *analytic work* of the framework, limit required coverage, and avoid making “maladaptation” seem like a theme that must be extensively documented by every reading.
> 
> Here is a tighter revision:
> 
> > ## Smallholder Adaptation Pathways under Climate Risk
> >
> > Smallholder households facing climate risk may adjust agricultural production, diversify livelihoods, migrate, or reallocate land and labor across activities and places.  
> >
> > **Drawing on the literature, develop a conceptual framework that links climate risks, household and local conditions, adaptation choices, and socioeconomic and environmental outcomes.** Your framework should explain how differences in the character of climate risk—for example, gradual climatic change versus acute shocks—and in households’ assets, opportunities, and constraints can produce divergent adaptation pathways.
> >
> > **Select and analyze three or four mechanisms or conditioning factors** that are central to your framework. These might include the distinction between ex ante investment and ex post coping; migration and remittances; labor availability and gender norms; technology adoption; or credit, insurance, land-tenure, and information frictions. Explain how the selected mechanisms interact and how they shape decisions and outcomes.
> >
> > **Under what conditions can adaptation support resilient and productive agricultural transformation, and when can it instead reproduce inequality, shift risks onto others, or create environmental harm?**
> 
> A few reasons I think this works better:
> 
> - The framework now has a defined object:  
>   \[
>   \text{climate risk} \rightarrow \text{conditions and constraints} \rightarrow \text{adaptation choices} \rightarrow \text{social and environmental outcomes}.
>   \]
>   It is not simply asking for a taxonomy of adaptation strategies.
> - “Select and analyze three or four” makes depth an explicit expectation. The parenthetical examples guide without requiring coverage.
> - “Divergent adaptation pathways” signals that the student should explain heterogeneity—not assume climate exposure mechanically determines behavior.
> - The final sentence broadens the concern beyond the technical term *maladaptation*, which is useful because the literature addresses several related but not identical ideas.
> 
> On your last point: **Diniega and Sakdapolrak is the reading most directly concerned with environmental maladaptation**, particularly through the transfer of practices and knowledge via social remittances, possible biodiversity loss, and the spread of practices poorly suited to a changing local context. But the concern is present, at least implicitly, in several other readings:
> 
> - **Davis and Lopez-Carr**: remittance-supported land acquisition and cattle ranching can be a privately rational response to labor scarcity and risk, while driving land-demand and degradation concerns rather than productive or sustainable intensification.
> - **Williams and Paudel**: migration may relax financial constraints through remittances while simultaneously reducing labor available for conservation adoption. This is a tradeoff rather than necessarily maladaptation, but it can produce environmentally adverse outcomes.
> - **Kaczan and Orgill-Meyer**: climate shocks can constrain mobility for resource-poor households, leaving some people exposed or “trapped”; selective migration can therefore produce unequal adaptive capacity.
> - **Afridi et al.**: gendered constraints in mobility and nonfarm diversification mean that seemingly household-level adaptation opportunities are distributed unequally within households and communities.
> - **Carleton et al.**: explicitly discuss externalities and policy-induced distortions—including pecuniary externalities associated with selective migration and moral hazard associated with insurance or aid. These are not always “maladaptation” in the Diniega–Sakdapolrak sense, but they strongly support the question’s broader concern with outcomes that shift costs to others.
> - **Hultgren et al.**: emphasize unequal residual exposure and adaptation across regions. Again, this is not an account of maladaptive action per se, but it supports the claim that adaptation does not imply equitable or adequate outcomes.
> 
> So I would avoid requiring students to establish a large literature on *maladaptation* narrowly defined. The phrase **“reproduce inequality, shift risks onto others, or create environmental harm”** better matches the full reading list. It permits a student to discuss maladaptive technology or land-use change where relevant, while also engaging migration selectivity, gendered constraint, land-price externalities, and incomplete adaptation.
