> These work well as a compact pair: **Question 1** tests substantive theorizing about heterogeneous pathways and outcomes; **Question 2** tests risk characterization, causal inference, and methodological judgment. I would make only modest edits for flow and precision:
> 
> 1. **Smallholder households facing climate risk may adjust agricultural production, diversify livelihoods, migrate, or reallocate land and labor across activities and places. Drawing on the literature, develop a conceptual framework linking climate risks, household and local conditions, adaptation choices, and socioeconomic and environmental outcomes. Explain how variation in the character of climate risk—for example, gradual climatic change versus acute shocks—and in households’ assets, opportunities, and constraints can produce divergent adaptation pathways. Discuss the empirical and theoretical basis, scope of applicability, and implications of your framework.**
> 
> 2. **Smallholder farmers globally face increasing physical climate risks and exhibit differing patterns of adaptation, technological adoption, and crop switching or redistribution. Yet characterizing relevant climate risks and estimating their influence on farmer decision-making are challenging, particularly given indirect pathways involving migration and remittances, market frictions, and long-run socioeconomic change. Discuss the methodological challenges of assessing the returns to, and limits of, adaptation. Critically compare leading methodological approaches for addressing these challenges and assess what each can—and cannot—reveal about these complex human–environment interactions.**
> 
> A few specific judgments:
> 
> - I prefer **“smallholder”** rather than “small-holder” throughout.
> - In Question 1, adding “**empirical and theoretical basis, scope of applicability**” makes “basis, applicability, and implications” more concrete without specifying a long checklist of mechanisms.
> - “**Their influence on farmer decision-making**” avoids the slightly awkward repetition in “estimating its effects and its role in influencing.”
> - “**Returns to, and limits of, adaptation**” is sharper than “adaptation returns and limitations.” It leaves room to discuss yield, income, welfare, resilience, distributional, and environmental returns—not only private financial returns.
> - The final clause of Question 2—“**what each can—and cannot—reveal**”—encourages real comparison rather than a catalogue of methods. It should prompt discussion of, for example, reduced-form panel designs, long differences, experimental intervention studies, instruments or other quasi-experimental approaches, spatial/remote-sensing integration, qualitative work, and structural or process-based models, without requiring all of them.
> 
> The questions also have a productive relationship:
> 
> \[
> \text{Question 1: What pathways connect risk to outcomes, and why do they differ?}
> \]
> 
> \[
> \text{Question 2: How can those pathways and their consequences be credibly observed and estimated?}
> \]
> 
> That is a strong, non-overlapping two-question structure.
